ultramodern: do not write a blocked thread's state twice

0009 set OSThreadState::BLOCKED in do_send and do_recv, just after putting the
thread on the message queue's own wait list. The working tree has not had those
two lines for some time and nothing wrote down why, so this records the tree as
it has actually been running: every measurement in PLAN.md, including all of the
comparisons against the reference console, was taken without them.

What sets the state instead is the code that stops and starts threads, which is
where the state is also read -- osStopThread leaves a thread's queue name on it
and osStartThread puts it back on that queue and marks it blocked there. A patch
series that does not reproduce the tree it was tested on is worse than one line
of redundancy either way.

--- a/ultramodern/src/mesgqueue.cpp
+++ b/ultramodern/src/mesgqueue.cpp
@@ -112,7 +112,6 @@
         while (MQ_IS_FULL(mq)) {
             debug_printf("[Message Queue] Thread %d is blocked on send\n", TO_PTR(OSThread, ultramodern::this_thread())->id);
             ultramodern::thread_queue_insert(PASS_RDRAM GET_MEMBER(OSMesgQueue, mq_, blocked_on_send), ultramodern::this_thread());
-            TO_PTR(OSThread, ultramodern::this_thread())->state = OSThreadState::BLOCKED;
             ultramodern::run_next_thread_and_wait(PASS_RDRAM1);
         }
     }
@@ -151,7 +150,6 @@
         while (MQ_IS_EMPTY(mq)) {
             debug_printf("[Message Queue] Thread %d is blocked on receive\n", TO_PTR(OSThread, ultramodern::this_thread())->id);
             ultramodern::thread_queue_insert(PASS_RDRAM GET_MEMBER(OSMesgQueue, mq_, blocked_on_recv), ultramodern::this_thread());
-            TO_PTR(OSThread, ultramodern::this_thread())->state = OSThreadState::BLOCKED;
             ultramodern::run_next_thread_and_wait(PASS_RDRAM1);
         }
     }
